  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 112 PM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTuesday begins the long term with continued dryslotting over much of \nNM, with showers still favoring the northwestern quadrant closed to \nthe H5 closed low over UT. This continues into Wednesday morning \nbefore another vortmax rounds the southern periphery of the H5 low \nthrough northern NM. This will bring a Pacific cold front west to \neast across most of the state with any attendant showers favoring \nthe northern mountains with a light snow dusting or perhaps an inch \nor two atop the highest peaks. A Fall crisp will greet morning goers \nby Thursday morning with northern valley areas potentially seeing \ntheir first freezes of the season. \n\nThereafter, numerical model and AI model solutions are coming into \nmuch better agreement regarding the dynamic evolution of the H5 low. \nThe core portion of the H5 low looks to get picked up and ejected \nout by the polar jet over the northern Great Plains with a small \nsection of it being pinched off over northern NM, closing off and \nretrograding westward toward southern AZ by Thursday evening. \nThis will yield a cold front backing south and westward through \neastern NM, now favored to advance thru the gaps of the central \nmountain chain thru to western NM Thursday night and Friday \nmorning. Uncertainty arises regarding how much moisture there will\nbe advected northward into the TX Big Bend and Caprock regions. \nThere is considerable uncertainty now regarding TC Simon's \nforecast track, either shift southwestward and moving offshore, or\nhaving its remnants pulled northward into the aforementioned \nregions. Coverage and intensity of precipitation through \nsoutheastern NM for Friday and into next weekend will greatly \ndepend on how that unfolds.",
